      "source_sha256": "sha256:8255b058fed2a25310452e291183226538a28fc48ff2f25045386b081dc39c12",
      "source_text": "{\"passages\":\"passage 1:A hole or rupture in the eardrum, a thin membrane that separates the ear canal and the middle ear, is called a perforated eardrum. The medical term for eardrum is tympanic membrane.nfections can cause a hole in the eardrum as a side effect of otitis media. Symptoms of acuteotitis media (middle ear fluid with signs of infection) include a senese of fullness in the ear, some hearing loss, pain, and fever.\\n\\npassage 2:Causes of a ruptured, or perforated, eardrum may include: 1  Middle ear infection (otitis media). 2  A middle ear infection often results in the accumulation of fluids in your middle ear.3  Pressure from these fluids can cause the eardrum to rupture. Foreign objects in your ear. 2  Small objects, such as a cotton swab or hairpin, can puncture or tear the eardrum. 3  Severe head trauma. 4  Severe injury, such as skull fracture, may cause the dislocation or damage to middle and inner ear structures, including your eardrum.\\n\\npassage 3:A perforated eardrum or punctured eardrum is a rupture or perforation (hole) of the eardrum which can occur as a result of otitis media (ear infection), trauma (e.g. by trying to clean the ear with sharp instruments), explosion, loud noise or surgery (accidental creation of a rupture). perforated eardrum or punctured eardrum is a rupture or perforation (hole) of the eardrum which can occur as a result of otitis media (ear infection), trauma (e.g. by trying to clean the ear with sharp instruments), explosion, loud noise or surgery (accidental creation of a rupture).\\n\\n\",\"question\":\"what causes a ruptured eardrum\"}",
